6476f4b6 (1266 1793394155789 "guenchi" (("4gm4c2jr" . 3240) ("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f403") (src . "scope: a packet section keeps every one of its texts\n\nA review packet section carried (text a) (text b) and the reviewer\nreceived a only: read-packet's shape check took the first text entry\nand packet-sections copied that one, so a second text was dropped with\nno word in the answer. Two coverage pre-reviews lost their planned-rows\nsections this way before anyone noticed.\n\nA section may now carry any number of (text ...) entries. The section\nblock's text is their concatenation in packet order, one empty line\nbetween two; an entry with no value or with #f carries no text and is\npassed over, as it was; an empty string is a text; a value that is not\na string refuses the packet as packet-malformed, wherever it stands,\nbefore anything is made. The grammar comment and the README say so.\n\nRows in the review-channel fixture: a two-text section arrives whole,\nbyte for byte; three texts keep their order; one text and no text are\nas before (the existing row is the control); absent entries drop out\nbefore, between and after texts, an empty string stays, a child section\njoins its own; a non-string text in the second position, or in a\nchild's third, refuses the whole packet naming the file and makes no\ndirectory. Mutants read red on those rows and nothing else: the first\ntext only, a join with no separator, reversed order, the check on the\nfirst text only, the absent-value filter removed, an absent value read\nas the empty string. rows-baseline.txt carries the fixture's new hash\nand counts.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 552))))
